WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.240
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.820
Welcome to Eyes on
Enterprise, where

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.590
I'll be bringing on
my lovely colleagues

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.330
to talk about the
tech, strategies,

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.060
and best practices that are
helping to shape enterprises

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:22.850
today.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.850
My name is Stephanie
Wong, developer advocate.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.000
And today I have Sam Stoelinga
on the show, customer engineer.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.540
And we're going to be talking
about hybrid and multi-cloud

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.870
and how to approach that.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.095
So Sam, thanks for
being on the show today.

00:00:35.095 --> 00:00:36.500
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
thank you for having me.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.542
Happy to talk about hybrid
and multi-cloud today.

00:00:38.542 --> 00:00:39.710
And yeah, let's get started.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:42.130
STEPHANIE WONG: First off, no
large enterprise, no matter how

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.290
well prepared, can simply
move to the cloud in one

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.030
fell swoop, even
if their goal is

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.550
to migrate completely
to the cloud, hence

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.900
multi-cloud and hybrid.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.390
So can you help us
define both of those

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.610
in the context of an enterprise?

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.410
SAM STOELINGA:
Workloads and processes

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.687
are unique across
different enterprises.

00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:01.270
And that's why you also see the
term hybrid and multi-cloud be

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.520
used inconsistently.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.422
So let's start
with hybrid cloud.

00:01:05.422 --> 00:01:06.880
So hybrid cloud is
really where you

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.300
have a private,
on-prem data center,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.090
and you're using a public
cloud, and how can you

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.430
have workloads that
are interconnected

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.810
and be deployed across these
two different environments.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.120
Multi-cloud, on the other
hand, is really focused on what

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.330
if you have several cloud
providers, public clouds,

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.770
and also, possibly, a private
cloud or on-prem data center,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.963
and how can you use these
altogether for your workloads.

00:01:32.963 --> 00:01:34.630
Multi-cloud, on the
other hand, can also

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.298
mean if you'd only have
one cloud provider.

00:01:37.298 --> 00:01:39.340
But you want to make sure
that your workloads are

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.805
ready to be ported when needed.

00:01:40.805 --> 00:01:41.680
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.097
So what's interesting
here is it's

00:01:43.097 --> 00:01:45.970
more about the architectural
portability and readiness

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.010
of your workloads.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.760
And one other thing
you mentioned was it's

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.730
more about the extension of your
private network into the cloud

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.322
or from cloud to cloud.

00:01:53.322 --> 00:01:55.030
SAM STOELINGA: Yes,
that's one technology

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.050
to enable a hybrid cloud.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.840
So we have two
technologies that really

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.090
enable you to extend
your private data

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.800
center to the cloud,
where you can even

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.360
treat a cloud as
an additional rec

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.140
if you would like
to stay that way.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.020
So there is a
dedicated interconnect,

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.789
and there's the cloud VPN.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:13.540
And both allow you to [? span ?]
a private network that's

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.600
running on-prem and connect it
to the private network that's

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.630
running in Google Cloud,
which we call a VPC network.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.780
And that allows you to
advertise the on-prem network

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.820
to the cloud and the
cloud network to on-prem.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.930
So any users on-prem
can also directly access

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.560
any cloud service
that's exposed only

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.503
over to private VPC network.

00:02:34.503 --> 00:02:36.670
STEPHANIE WONG: Now, it's
clear that, at this point,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.045
there are a lot
of misconceptions

00:02:38.045 --> 00:02:39.470
about hybrid and multi-cloud.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.890
So I wanted to talk about
the top three misconceptions

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.720
that I've heard, and I want to
hear what you think of them.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.290
So the first one is that
there is a hybrid product that

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.540
makes my workloads hybrid.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.290
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
so I've had questions

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.940
where I would get
asked, like, what

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.350
is your hybrid cloud product?

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.530
And then they would
assume that there

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.060
is a product that
takes their workload

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:01.360
and makes it automatically
run across their private data

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.710
center and on the public cloud.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.100
And they would expect benefits
that they get out from it,

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.620
like lower cost, better
reliability, et cetera.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.110
However, I noticed that
I don't think there are

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.580
sorts of product that exist.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.790
Actually, hybrid cloud is more
about a set of technologies

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:19.620
that may or may not be adopted
by different kind of workloads.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.570
Also let's say we have a
batch processing workload.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.330
That workload [? needs to ?]
[? be ?] run every night.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.700
And it needs to be
finished within four hours

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.830
before the next day begins.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.890
The enterprise has
a hybrid strategy

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:35.740
that they want to
use their existing

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:40.130
investments for on-premise
as much as possible.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.820
So every workload
owner is encouraged

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.082
to use that first, but
for any excess capacity,

00:03:45.082 --> 00:03:47.290
they need to go to the cloud,
because they don't want

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.030
to invest more in
on-premise, but they

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.030
want to make sure to keep
the existing investments

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.570
and make the most out of them.

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.290
So what they do is they have
an interconnect and the burst

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:59.140
processing workloads spins
up more VMs in the cloud

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.510
and then it transfers--
it does some processing,

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.560
and it sends the
results back to on-prem.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.600
So the data stays on-prem.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.030
Some capacity is being
used from on-prem.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.450
But the majority for this
work needs to be in the cloud

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.040
because otherwise, they couldn't
finish within four hours.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.200
So it's a really good example of
one hybrid architecture that's

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.200
useful for this work.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.658
However, for other workloads
you might see a different kind

00:04:20.658 --> 00:04:21.769
of hybrid architecture.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:22.029
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.071
I think that's really
interesting, because again,

00:04:24.071 --> 00:04:26.080
it's more about the
feature and technology that

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.750
enables you to burst or
have better redundancy

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.340
by moving to the cloud.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.710
So I want to talk about my
second misconception, which

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.230
is that by moving
to the public cloud,

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.480
that inherently makes
your workloads publicly

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:38.740
facing to the internet.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.490
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
and, I mean, it comes

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.180
from the name of public cloud--

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.230
means everything has
to be public, right?

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:50.770
So I would say it's not unheard
of that people think that way.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.760
However, it's not--
that's not true.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.380
You can simply expose your
workloads over a VPC network

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.380
only and then bridge
that VPC network

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.380
if you're on-prem using the
interconnect or a cloud VPN

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.220
that we have talked
about previously.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.560
STEPHANIE WONG: Now for
the third misconception--

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.150
moving to hybrid and
multi-cloud means

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.070
moving everything at once.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.240
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.940
I think that really
comes from the fact

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.370
that people have
workloads that always

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.810
have many other dependencies.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.590
And these dependencies often
reside on-prem as well,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:20.565
so they can--

00:05:20.565 --> 00:05:23.310
they assume that the fact
that they move the workload,

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.792
they also need to move all
the dependencies with it.

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:27.000
However, that's not the case.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.210
Like we explained earlier, we
have hybrid cloud connectivity,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:33.230
which allows you to
move the workload

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.390
but still keep the
dependencies on-prem.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.057
STEPHANIE WONG: Do
you mean like running

00:05:37.057 --> 00:05:40.040
your legacy backend systems
on-premise or your database

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.020
on-premise and then
your Kubernetes Engine

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.400
front-end in the cloud?

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.025
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
I think that would

00:05:45.025 --> 00:05:47.550
be a great example of how you
could migrate your workloads

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:48.810
with all its dependencies.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:49.402
Yeah.

00:05:49.402 --> 00:05:51.110
STEPHANIE WONG: Let's
move on to deciding

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:55.670
on a strategy, because I know an
estimated 88% of organizations

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:57.620
are adopting multi-cloud today.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.405
And that can be for
many reasons, one

00:05:59.405 --> 00:06:01.280
being risk reduction--
they don't want to put

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.780
all their eggs in one basket.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.180
Another being
architectural similarity,

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.840
like for like between clouds,
or maybe even best of breed

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.300
or feature availability
in each cloud.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.070
Have you seen any other
motivating factors

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:13.998
for companies?

00:06:13.998 --> 00:06:16.040
SAM STOELINGA: So I think
you mostly captured it.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.130
Most customers, they want
to reduce the lock-in

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.480
they have to any vendor.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.500
They want to have the leverage
over the vendors versus losing

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:25.653
that altogether.

00:06:25.653 --> 00:06:28.070
And they want to be able to
choose the cloud provider that

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:29.870
provides them the best service.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.220
STEPHANIE WONG: What about
maintaining consistency

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.210
across environments?

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.810
You need consistent
tooling that's

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.160
simple that allows you to manage
things, like access policies,

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.830
and you might have dependencies
between applications and needs

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.538
for performance and
latency requirements.

00:06:41.538 --> 00:06:43.080
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
I know, exactly.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.500
And maintaining consistency
can be a challenge.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.390
That's also why Google has
several tools available,

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.930
such as containers,
Kubernetes, and Anthos

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:52.880
that allow you to
maintain consistency

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.720
across different environments.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:55.220
STEPHANIE WONG: Now
that we've talked

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.260
about the foundations of
hybrid and multi-cloud,

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:00.080
I do want to talk about
a specific case study.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.990
What are some typical use cases
in which performance, storage

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.380
cost, or modernization
of existing apps

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.960
were key reasons to migrate?

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.240
SAM STOELINGA: So yeah, I'm
working with this customer

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.200
today.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.750
They're trying to modernize
their [INAUDIBLE] on-prem IT

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:12.500
stack.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.080
And they're running
a CI/CD workload,

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.460
Jenkins, currently on-prem.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.770
And their technical
requirement is

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:23.550
where they need to be able to
access the on-prem artifact

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.820
storage, and the
on-prem users need

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:30.000
to be able to access the
Jenkins instance as well.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.460
So with these requirements,
they came to us and said,

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.010
we want GKE on-prem.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.635
STEPHANIE WONG: So
it was clear to them

00:07:35.635 --> 00:07:37.520
that they needed to
continue running Jenkins

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.160
but on GKE on-premise.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.240
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah, well,
that's what they told,

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.430
but then we started
talking more and more.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.260
And we found out
that, hey, we actually

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:49.150
can run all these things,
matching these requirements,

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.990
while still running
it on the cloud.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:55.310
So we introduced this
customer to several features,

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.650
one of them being
GKE private masters

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.800
and private nodes, which
allows you to run GKE clusters

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.630
in a fully private fashion.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.840
So none of the GKE workloads
are exposed publicly.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.170
The GKE nodes themselves do not
even have a public IP address.

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:13.890
Everything is directly available
over the private network,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:15.685
which is connected to on-prem.

00:08:15.685 --> 00:08:17.060
And at the same
time, this allows

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.430
them to run Jenkins
on top of GKE

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.280
while still being able to
access the artifact storage.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.180
So in this way, we had a hybrid
solution for this customer

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.370
by moving part of
their CI/CD stack

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.828
to the cloud, while still being
able to connect to on-prem.

00:08:30.828 --> 00:08:32.287
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's interesting.

00:08:32.287 --> 00:08:33.813
And I think it
clarifies that you

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:35.480
do need to have a
product and networking

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.840
understanding to know that you
can actually restrict access

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.507
to public endpoints for
a private GKE cluster.

00:08:41.507 --> 00:08:42.590
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah, yeah.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:44.750
Networking experience
is always needed.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.530
Even people think they
move to the cloud,

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:48.530
they don't need network
engineers anymore.

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.925
But networking knowledge is
required, even in the cloud.

00:08:51.925 --> 00:08:54.050
STEPHANIE WONG: So let's
talk about migrations now.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:57.362
In most cases, migrations is a
one-time, irreversible effort.

00:08:57.362 --> 00:08:58.820
But with hybrid
and multi-cloud, it

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.900
makes it so that you
can move in phases now.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:02.653
So I think portability
here is key.

00:09:02.653 --> 00:09:04.070
But let's say I'm
an organization,

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.660
I want to make my
workloads portable,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:07.737
what would be my
first step to do that?

00:09:07.737 --> 00:09:09.320
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
so the first step

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:10.862
is to make sure that
your workload is

00:09:10.862 --> 00:09:14.620
able to shift from one computing
platform to another platform.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.180
In order to do that, you need
to separate the application

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.320
deployment and management
from the artifact of how you

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.700
delivered the software itself.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.600
So there are many tools
that help you do that today.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.380
And based on these tools,
we can form a tool chain

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:34.670
that helps you do this for any
VM or container-based workload

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:35.790
that you might have.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:37.290
So I think key here
is understanding

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.625
that making your
workload portable

00:09:39.625 --> 00:09:41.750
doesn't mean that you have
to make it cloud native.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:47.075
This is often a effort that
is not significant to do.

00:09:47.075 --> 00:09:49.700
And people can start doing that
today with the tools available.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:50.900
STEPHANIE WONG: OK,
let's get specific.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:52.650
Can you give me an
example of a tool chain

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:55.370
that you can use to build VM
images and deploy to any cloud

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:55.940
then?

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:58.482
SAM STOELINGA: There are a few
tools critical to start a tool

00:09:58.482 --> 00:10:02.330
chain, mostly for, I would
say, there is a CI system,

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:05.930
there is the image builder,
there's a source version

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:06.980
control system.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:09.260
And all this together
form a tool chain

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.420
that allows you to
build images that can be

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.800
deployed across any platform.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.440
So let me give an
example of how you

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.850
would establish, like,
what tools would you

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:17.990
use for this tool chain.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:19.365
So for the Image
Builder, there's

00:10:19.365 --> 00:10:21.230
a great open source
tool by HashiCorp,

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:24.575
which is called Packer, which
takes in the application

00:10:24.575 --> 00:10:27.470
deployment logic
and a base image

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.560
like, for example, Ubuntu or
another Linux system like RHEL,

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:33.440
and then builds an image
that can be deployed

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.310
across different platforms.

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.910
But it's always built using
the same deployment script.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.580
So this really allows you
to build deployable images

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.610
for any cloud platform
or on-premise system.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.500
The other system
is the CI system

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:49.760
which job is really taking the
latest commit from the source

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.520
version control
system and building

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:56.540
an image using the latest
application software.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:00.080
And tying these together
allows you to build a pipeline

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:02.270
that you can now build
images automatically

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:05.267
for any platform whenever
a new commit gets

00:11:05.267 --> 00:11:07.100
pushed to the source
version control system.

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.960
So that's really--
that's a simple example

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.230
of how you would do it.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:12.060
And most customers,
they should choose what

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:13.310
they're already familiar with.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:15.218
So if you're using
today Jenkins for CI,

00:11:15.218 --> 00:11:16.510
you should continue using that.

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:17.570
However, if you're using
[? another tool, ?] you

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:19.610
can easily plug and
play different tools

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:20.907
to build such a pipeline.

00:11:20.907 --> 00:11:22.490
STEPHANIE WONG: That
tool chain sounds

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.070
really great for workloads
that have an automated way

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:26.840
to deploy applications in VMs.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.532
But what if I have an
application in a VM already

00:11:29.532 --> 00:11:31.240
and I wanted to deploy
that to the cloud?

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:32.865
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
so that's actually

00:11:32.865 --> 00:11:34.370
a pretty common
scenario, especially

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.720
for a large enterprise that buy
their software from a vendor,

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.710
and that vendor gave
them a VMware image,

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:42.770
and that VMware image doesn't
have the applications separated

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:44.300
from the image itself.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.758
So they have no way of taking
the application and deploying

00:11:46.758 --> 00:11:49.342
it [? on a ?] [? base image ?]
that can be deployed to another

00:11:49.342 --> 00:11:49.880
cloud.

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:53.900
So the only solution is to
take that VM as is and somehow

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:55.190
get it to the cloud.

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:57.740
So there are two solutions
that Google Cloud offers today

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:58.480
to customers.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:01.550
There's Migrate for Compute
Engine, which was previously

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:05.390
known as Velostrata, which
allows you to take a VM as is

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.700
and migrate it to the cloud.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.510
And it has a lot
of cool features.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:12.830
You can even migrate a VM,
[INAUDIBLE] in the original VM,

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:15.150
and test whether the
migration was successful.

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:19.380
So you can do a low risk
VM migration to the cloud.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.500
The other solution
that I mentioned

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:24.710
is Anthos Migrate, which
allows you to take a VM

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.170
and convert it to
a container and run

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:29.300
that container in the cloud.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:32.210
So both solutions allow
you to move workloads

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:35.300
from on-prem or out-of-cloud
providers to Google Cloud.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.770
STEPHANIE WONG: So when would
you use one over the other

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:37.310
then?

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:38.810
SAM STOELINGA: The
two solutions are

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:40.820
different in what kind
of use case it supports.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:44.690
So Anthos Migrate is a
solution that takes of a VM

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:46.320
and converts it to container.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.360
And because it's a more
sophisticated technology,

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.005
it's more limited into what
kind of source workloads

00:12:52.005 --> 00:12:53.930
it can support.

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:56.600
Migrate for Compute
Engine, on the other hand,

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.220
allows you to migrate much
bigger range of workloads.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:03.678
I would say it support most
workloads that you can imagine.

00:13:03.678 --> 00:13:05.220
STEPHANIE WONG: In
these cases, we're

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.160
talking about migrating
existing VM workloads.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.200
But what if I want
to create a standard

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.630
for creating portable
applications that we

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.730
can move to any cloud?

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:16.190
SAM STOELINGA: So that's
really where containers fit in.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.220
Containers really
forces everyone

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:22.280
within the company to
have a standard that

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.740
allows you to deploy the
container the same way

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:26.820
in any environment.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:30.530
However, if you start
looking at how do I

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:33.140
manage a multi-host
environment, you

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:34.670
get into a more
sophisticated area.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:36.503
And that's really where
Kubernetes comes in.

00:13:36.503 --> 00:13:38.480
So Kubernetes solves
the chance of how can I

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:42.320
schedule containers across
a host, a set of host

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:43.340
in a cluster.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:45.780
How can I do low balancing
across these containers?

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.250
How do I ensure that I
can scale out when needed?

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.750
STEPHANIE WONG: Kubernetes has
become the de facto standard,

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:53.247
but is there any difference
in developer experience?

00:13:53.247 --> 00:13:54.080
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:54.640
Definitely.

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:55.640
And that's a good thing.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.860
I think improvement in
developer experience

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:59.700
is always a good thing to have.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.060
So developers can
really focus on writing

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.790
the code versus worrying about
where their code works well

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:07.670
in production and in
development because they have

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:09.530
the same container
that they build

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.810
that also gets
used in development

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:14.130
but also gets used
in production.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:18.020
So they're able to improve
their production reliability

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:20.510
by having this kind of
developer experience.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:21.350
STEPHANIE WONG: I think it
would be helpful to jump

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:22.830
into another scenario.

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.563
Let's say that I am
a company, and I've

00:14:24.563 --> 00:14:26.480
containerized some
applications, but I'm still

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.240
primarily using monolithic
applications on-premise.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.460
So how can I use Google tools
to help modernize and port

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:33.100
to the cloud?

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.100
SAM STOELINGA: We have a
modernization platform,

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:36.800
which we call
Anthos, which allows

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:40.380
you to modernize your
applications either on-prem

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.090
or in the cloud.

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:42.470
It's based on open platform.

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:45.970
So it says Kubernetes,
which we call Anthos GKE;

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.350
STO, which we call
Anthos Service Mesh;

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:51.320
and Knative, which
we call Cloud Run.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:57.680
And these technologies really
allow you to modernize in place

00:14:57.680 --> 00:14:59.510
and move to the cloud
when you're ready.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:01.552
STEPHANIE WONG: What does
Anthos look like for IT

00:15:01.552 --> 00:15:02.780
ops versus developers?

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:06.043
What's the user journey like,
when it comes to migrating?

00:15:06.043 --> 00:15:08.210
SAM STOELINGA: So I've been
involved in a few Anthos

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:09.140
deployments.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:12.200
And all of the cases
that I've been involved,

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:15.290
both developers and the
IT team have been engaged.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:18.150
Actually, not just IT-- the
networking team, security team,

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:19.940
which you could
consider part of IT,

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:22.040
they've all been super
engaged, especially

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.170
during the initial
installation, but also

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:27.170
afterwards for making
sure that the Kubernetes

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:30.560
platform, the Anthos
platform stays running and up

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:33.450
and making sure the develop
have a proper experience.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:35.230
IT operations are
always going to be

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:37.485
involved in those scenarios.

00:15:37.485 --> 00:15:38.860
And developers,
on the other end,

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:43.450
are going to be focused on
making sure their workloads get

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.340
up and running
successfully on Kubernetes

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.150
and make sure they
stay up and running.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.150
So they have different
roles, but they are still

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:50.200
both fully engaged.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:50.770
Yeah.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:52.270
STEPHANIE WONG: To
wrap up, what are

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:55.900
some of your views on the trends
for hybrid and multi-cloud?

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:57.400
SAM STOELINGA: Yeah,
so I have seen,

00:15:57.400 --> 00:15:59.650
no matter which kind of
customer I work with,

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:02.020
you often see them
standardizing on Kubernetes

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:05.900
as the platform for workloads
to be running for the future,

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.260
but also for current
workers to be migrated to.

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:11.530
And even industries that are
traditionally been slower

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:13.030
to adopt newer
technologies, they're

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:14.440
also starting to
adopt Kubernetes

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:17.380
as the standard for their
internal applications

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.500
and external or any application.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:20.000
Yeah.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.430
STEPHANIE WONG: So for
those people in that bucket,

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:24.100
what can they do to get started
and get some value out of cloud

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:25.010
today?

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:27.550
SAM STOELINGA: I think that the
biggest thing they should do

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:30.010
is just get started
with a smaller pilot,

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:33.190
like find a workload that they
want to modernize right now,

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:36.460
get it running on Kubernetes,
see what benefits they

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:39.530
gain from doing that, and repeat
it for other workloads as well.

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:42.070
But really, sometimes it's more
of, just get it done and get

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:43.190
started.

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:44.620
STEPHANIE WONG: Sam, thank you
so much for being on the show.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.412
SAM STOELINGA: No,
thank you for having me.

00:16:46.412 --> 00:16:47.140
It was fun.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:47.740
STEPHANIE WONG:
Everyone, check out

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:50.450
the articles on hybrid and
multi-cloud at the links below.

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:52.752
And comment with your own
experience with it as well.

00:16:52.752 --> 00:16:54.460
Join us next time for
Eyes on Enterprise.

